      <node id="mtg-security-2026-09-29@1.0#weekly-security-meeting-29-september-2026/exception-review-tomorrow/1" label="mtg-security-2026-09-29@1.0#weekly-security-meeting-29-september-2026/exception-review-tomorrow/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Weekly Security Meeting, 29 September 2026 | Weekly Security Meeting, 29 September 2026 &gt; Exception review tomorrow&#10;There are still two approved accounts whose holders can sign in without an MFA challenge. Daniel Hughes (daniel.hughes) has the lab restriction, because the authenticator app cannot be installed on the workstation used for that work. Amelia Ward (amelia.ward) has the emergency administrator arrangement for the identity provider, allowing recovery when the MFA service is unavailable.&#10;&#10;Thomas confirmed the position discussed earlier this month: both exceptions continue through the end of the year and need review by 2026-12-31. The quarterly review of the MFA Exception Register is booked for 2026-09-30. Isabel will complete the register checks with Thomas tomorrow and ensure the recorded reasons still match the approved needs.&#10;&#10;The register review is a separate completion step. Today's notes should not be treated as evidence that tomorrow's check has already been done." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-09-29&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;mtg-security-2026-09-29&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;meeting&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;weekly-security-meeting-29-september-2026/exception-review-tomorrow&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Weekly Security Meeting, 29 September 2026&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="mtg-security-2026-09-29@1.0#weekly-security-meeting-29-september-2026/self-service-password-reset/1" label="mtg-security-2026-09-29@1.0#weekly-security-meeting-29-september-2026/self-service-password-reset/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Weekly Security Meeting, 29 September 2026 | Weekly Security Meeting, 29 September 2026 &gt; Self-service password reset&#10;The planned launch date is 2026-10-19. The portal will be on the intranet. Joseph's staff notice went out on 2026-09-28 and says that resets continue through the helpdesk until then. The team agreed to keep that message consistent in conversations with staff.&#10;&#10;Before launch, Ruby will check the support wording for people who cannot finish the self-service steps. A launch date does not remove the need for identity checks or change the current password requirements. Any delay will need a clear staff update." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-09-29&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;mtg-security-2026-09-29&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;meeting&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;weekly-security-meeting-29-september-2026/self-service-password-reset&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Weekly Security Meeting, 29 September 2026&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="mtg-security-2026-09-29@1.0#weekly-security-meeting-29-september-2026/actions-and-close/1" label="mtg-security-2026-09-29@1.0#weekly-security-meeting-29-september-2026/actions-and-close/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Weekly Security Meeting, 29 September 2026 | Weekly Security Meeting, 29 September 2026 &gt; Actions and close&#10;Isabel owns the register review on 2026-09-30. Joseph and Ruby own the self-service support guidance, due for review on 2026-10-12. Freya will keep the overseas travel reminder in the next manager briefing. Thomas asked everyone to record completed actions explicitly so that planned work is not mistaken for finished work." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-09-29&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;mtg-security-2026-09-29&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;meeting&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;weekly-security-meeting-29-september-2026/actions-and-close&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Weekly Security Meeting, 29 September 2026&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/purpose/1" label="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/purpose/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Acceptable Use Policy | Acceptable Use Policy &gt; Purpose&#10;Company equipment and access are provided for company work. Use them carefully, respect other people's information and leave systems available for colleagues who need them. This policy applies in both offices, at home and when travelling. It covers company laptops, shared machines, email, internet use and information handled through company accounts.&#10;&#10;People owns this policy. Managers should explain it during induction and answer ordinary questions about reasonable use. IT Support handles equipment and access problems. Security should be contacted where the concern is possible misuse, disclosure or an unexpected request involving an account." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-01-05&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;HR&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-acceptable-use&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;People&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;acceptable-use-policy/purpose&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Acceptable Use Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/looking-after-equipment/1" label="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/looking-after-equipment/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Acceptable Use Policy | Acceptable Use Policy &gt; Looking after equipment&#10;Use the company laptop assigned to you and take reasonable care of it. Keep food and drinks away from equipment where a spill could cause damage. Carry laptops securely when moving between locations. Do not leave equipment unattended in public areas or visible in an unattended vehicle.&#10;&#10;Do not attempt a physical repair yourself. Report damage to the helpdesk and describe the problem clearly. IT Support will decide whether to repair or replace the laptop. If a replacement is issued, complete the handover and return the old laptop to IT. Do not lend the old laptop to a colleague or keep it for occasional use.&#10;&#10;Do not install unapproved software or change security settings. If a tool is needed for work, explain the requirement to IT Support. Do not use someone else's administrative account to install it. A business need should be handled through the normal approval process rather than an improvised change." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-01-05&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;HR&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-acceptable-use&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;People&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;acceptable-use-policy/looking-after-equipment&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Acceptable Use Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/accounts-and-shared-machines/1" label="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/accounts-and-shared-machines/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Acceptable Use Policy | Acceptable Use Policy &gt; Accounts and shared machines&#10;Use only your own account. Passwords, authenticator approvals and recovery codes must not be shared. A colleague's absence is not permission to sign in as them. Ask the relevant system owner to arrange approved access when work needs to be covered.&#10;&#10;Shared machines are shared equipment, not shared identities. Sign out at the end of your session so the next user does not work under your account. Do not leave confidential material visible on the screen or local working documents available to the next person. Follow the shared device arrangements and tell the helpdesk if the listed users or location have changed.&#10;&#10;Check the recipient before sending information and the audience before placing it in a shared area. All Staff access to the file share does not make all information suitable for general circulation. Use access controls and the system owner's guidance for confidential or restricted material." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-01-05&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;HR&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-acceptable-use&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;People&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;acceptable-use-policy/accounts-and-shared-machines&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Acceptable Use Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/email-and-internet-use/1" label="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/email-and-internet-use/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Acceptable Use Policy | Acceptable Use Policy &gt; Email and internet use&#10;Write company email with the same care as other business correspondence. Do not send abusive, discriminatory, threatening or deliberately misleading messages. Do not use company email to impersonate someone or to commit the company to arrangements you are not authorised to make.&#10;&#10;Treat unexpected attachments and requests for passwords with caution. Never enter a password after clicking an email link. Open the normal sign-in route yourself if you need to check an account. Do not approve an MFA request you did not initiate. Report suspicious messages to Security without circulating them to colleagues for informal opinions.&#10;&#10;Use internet access for lawful work and reasonable personal activity. Do not deliberately access unlawful material, attempt unauthorised access to other people's information or use company equipment to interfere with someone else's work. Avoid downloads that have no work purpose and could create security or capacity problems." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-01-05&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;HR&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-acceptable-use&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;People&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;acceptable-use-policy/email-and-internet-use&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Acceptable Use Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/reasonable-personal-use/1" label="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/reasonable-personal-use/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Acceptable Use Policy | Acceptable Use Policy &gt; Reasonable personal use&#10;Limited personal use is allowed when it does not interfere with duties, create significant cost or expose company information. It must not become a separate business activity or prevent another person from using shared equipment. Managers may ask staff to reduce personal use where it affects work.&#10;&#10;Personal phones may be used to read email. They are not registered company devices. This limited permission does not allow other internal systems to be accessed from those phones. Do not use personal storage or personal email to move company work between devices." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-01-05&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;HR&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-acceptable-use&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;People&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;acceptable-use-policy/reasonable-personal-use&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Acceptable Use Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/loss-mistakes-and-concerns/1" label="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/loss-mistakes-and-concerns/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Acceptable Use Policy | Acceptable Use Policy &gt; Loss, mistakes and concerns&#10;Report a lost device to the helpdesk the same day. Give the device name, when and where it was last seen and whether it was in use. Do not wait to finish searching before making contact. Provide further details when they become available.&#10;&#10;Report accidental disclosure or possible account misuse promptly to Security. Explain what you observed and what you have already done. Do not conceal a mistake or make unsupported claims about its impact. Early, factual information helps the company decide what action is needed." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-01-05&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;HR&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-acceptable-use&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;People&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;acceptable-use-policy/loss-mistakes-and-concerns&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Acceptable Use Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/consequences-and-advice/1" label="pol-acceptable-use@4.2#acceptable-use-policy/consequences-and-advice/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Acceptable Use Policy | Acceptable Use Policy &gt; Consequences and advice&#10;The company may restrict access while suspected misuse is assessed. People will consider the circumstances, seriousness and any repeated conduct when deciding an appropriate response. Deliberate or serious misuse may lead to disciplinary action. Equipment problems and honest mistakes should still be reported promptly.&#10;&#10;If a request seems inconsistent with this policy, ask your manager, People or Security before carrying it out. An urgent instruction does not remove the requirement to protect accounts and company information." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-01-05&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;HR&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-acceptable-use&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;People&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;acceptable-use-policy/consequences-and-advice&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Acceptable Use Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/setting-up-the-authenticator-app/1" label="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/setting-up-the-authenticator-app/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Multi-Factor Authentication Policy | Multi-Factor Authentication Policy &gt; Setting up the authenticator app&#10;Follow the enrolment instructions available through the intranet or ask the helpdesk to guide you. Install the authenticator app using the approved instructions, then start enrolment from the usual identity provider sign-in route. Do not begin enrolment from a link in an unexpected message.&#10;&#10;Complete the pairing step privately and test the app before leaving the setup process. The test should be a sign-in you started yourself. If the test does not work, stop and contact the helpdesk. Do not repeatedly approve prompts to see whether one of them completes setup.&#10;&#10;The helpdesk can explain the process without asking for your password or a recovery code. If you change the phone used for the app, arrange the change while you still have access where possible. Do not remove your existing working method before the replacement has been tested." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-02-23&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-mfa&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;multi-factor-authentication-policy/setting-up-the-authenticator-app&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Multi-Factor Authentication Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/purpose-and-scope/1" label="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/purpose-and-scope/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Access Control Policy | Access Control Policy &gt; Purpose and scope&#10;Access follows the work a person needs to do. Being employed by the company does not give someone permission to administer systems or approve payments. This policy covers accounts and access to company information in the UK office, the US office and remote working locations. It applies to employees, system owners and anyone handling an access request.&#10;&#10;Use the smallest set of permissions that allows the work to be completed. A useful permission is not automatically a necessary permission. Access for a temporary assignment must have an end date. The requester must describe the work, the systems involved and the duration before approval is sought." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-12&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-access-control&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;access-control-policy/purpose-and-scope&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Access Control Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/baseline-and-privileged-access/1" label="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/baseline-and-privileged-access/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Access Control Policy | Access Control Policy &gt; Baseline and privileged access&#10;Every member of staff belongs to All Staff. This provides access to email, intranet and file share. Permission to use the file share does not permit unrestricted distribution of confidential material. Staff must still follow the handling instructions for the information they use.&#10;&#10;Privileged access is provided only through IT Admins, Finance Approvers and HR Data Owners. Do not create individual grants as a shortcut around group approval. The system owner must approve access to the relevant system before group membership is changed. Where one group reaches several systems, obtain approval from each affected system owner. A manager's request alone is not sufficient authority to make the change.&#10;&#10;The approved memberships are:&#10;&#10;| Group | Members |&#10;|---|---|&#10;| IT Admins | Amelia Ward (amelia.ward), Joseph Burton (joseph.burton), Leah Gibbons (leah.gibbons), Ruby Stevens (ruby.stevens) |&#10;| Finance Approvers | Hannah Porter (hannah.porter), Mark Sullivan (mark.sullivan), Max Thornton (max.thornton), William Ellis (william.ellis) |&#10;| HR Data Owners | Benjamin Cole (benjamin.cole), Chloe Barker (chloe.barker), Grace Mitchell (grace.mitchell) |&#10;&#10;Department membership does not replace these rules. Someone in Finance does not automatically become a Finance Approver. Someone outside Finance may have approved membership when their duties require it." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-12&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-access-control&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;access-control-policy/baseline-and-privileged-access&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Access Control Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/systems-and-accountable-owners/1" label="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/systems-and-accountable-owners/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Access Control Policy | Access Control Policy &gt; Systems and accountable owners&#10;| System | Sensitivity | System owner | Access group |&#10;|---|---|---|---|&#10;| email | internal | Joseph Burton | All Staff |&#10;| intranet | internal | Amelia Ward | All Staff |&#10;| file share | confidential | Amelia Ward | All Staff |&#10;| identity provider | restricted | Amelia Ward | IT Admins |&#10;| VPN gateway | confidential | Leah Gibbons | IT Admins |&#10;| domain controller | restricted | Ruby Stevens | IT Admins |&#10;| payments system | restricted | Hannah Porter | Finance Approvers |&#10;| ERP | confidential | Max Thornton | Finance Approvers |&#10;| HR system | restricted | Chloe Barker | HR Data Owners |&#10;&#10;For the identity provider, VPN gateway and domain controller, the group shown controls administrative access. All staff sign in through the identity provider. All staff may connect through the VPN gateway. These ordinary activities do not make the person an administrator. Owners must explain this distinction when approving requests so that support does not grant administrative permissions for routine sign-in or remote work." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-12&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-access-control&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;access-control-policy/systems-and-accountable-owners&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Access Control Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/joiners/1" label="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/joiners/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Access Control Policy | Access Control Policy &gt; Joiners&#10;People confirms a new starter's identity, department, office and starting date. IT Support issues an individual account and company laptop against that instruction. All Staff membership is the starting position. Any request for privileged membership follows the owner approval process separately.&#10;&#10;The helpdesk records what was requested, who approved it, who applied it and when it was checked. Dates and times in the record use UTC. Before closing the request, confirm that the person can reach the required systems and that no unrelated group was added. First sign-in includes the password and MFA setup required by the current policies." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-12&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-access-control&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;access-control-policy/joiners&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Access Control Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="reg-shared-devices@1.3#shared-device-register/register-details/1" label="reg-shared-devices@1.3#shared-device-register/register-details/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Shared Device Register | Shared Device Register &gt; Register details&#10;Maintained by Ruby Stevens (ruby.stevens), IT Support. Last updated 2026-06-15. This register covers shared company equipment, not the individual company laptops issued to staff. The listed users identify the people expected to use each machine for their work. They must still sign in with their own accounts.&#10;&#10;| Device | Location | Users |&#10;|---|---|---|&#10;| MR-PC-01 | UK office, second-floor meeting room | Adam Fraser (adam.fraser), George Palmer (george.palmer), Grace Mitchell (grace.mitchell) |&#10;| WH-TERM-01 | UK office, warehouse work area | Aisha Rahman (aisha.rahman), Harry Morgan (harry.morgan), Max Thornton (max.thornton), Finn Archer (finn.archer) |" />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-06-15&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;reg-shared-devices&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;register&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;shared-device-register/register-details&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Shared Device Register&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/movers-and-temporary-duties/1" label="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/movers-and-temporary-duties/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Access Control Policy | Access Control Policy &gt; Movers and temporary duties&#10;A change of role is a new access decision. The receiving manager describes the new duties; the previous manager identifies permissions that are no longer needed. IT Support removes unnecessary memberships as part of the move, rather than allowing permissions to accumulate across roles.&#10;&#10;Temporary membership must identify a person responsible for confirming its removal. If work continues beyond the agreed end date, obtain a fresh owner approval. Do not extend access merely because nobody has complained. Record both the new permission and any permission removed in the same request where practical." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-12&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-access-control&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;access-control-policy/movers-and-temporary-duties&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Access Control Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/leavers/1" label="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/leavers/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Access Control Policy | Access Control Policy &gt; Leavers&#10;People supplies the last working day and informs IT Support of any changes to it. Access must be removed on the last working day. Coordinate the timing with People so that an account does not remain usable after the agreed departure point. Disable sign-in, end active sessions and remove group memberships. Company equipment is returned through IT Support.&#10;&#10;The analyst records completion and checks that all listed systems have been covered. A returned laptop is not evidence that account access has ended. Escalate an incomplete removal to Security and the affected system owner immediately." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-12&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-access-control&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;access-control-policy/leavers&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Access Control Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/quarterly-review-and-exceptions/1" label="pol-access-control@2.1#access-control-policy/quarterly-review-and-exceptions/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Access Control Policy | Access Control Policy &gt; Quarterly review and exceptions&#10;Each quarter, system owners review membership against actual duties. IT Support provides the membership list and approval records. Owners confirm continuing need or instruct removal; silence is not approval. Security follows up missing responses and unresolved permissions.&#10;&#10;Thomas Price (thomas.price), who leads security, resolves disputes about unnecessary privilege with the owner. No one may approve their own access request. Changes must remain traceable to the person who requested, approved and implemented them. Access review supports M1018; the working requirement is the owner review and removal process described here." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-12&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-access-control&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;access-control-policy/quarterly-review-and-exceptions&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Access Control Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/requirement-and-effective-date/1" label="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/requirement-and-effective-date/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Multi-Factor Authentication Policy | Multi-Factor Authentication Policy &gt; Requirement and effective date&#10;From 2026-03-02, every account must use multi-factor authentication, referred to as MFA. The standard method is an authenticator app. This applies to staff in the UK office and the US office, remote staff and accounts with administrative permissions. A password alone does not meet the standard unless a named exception has been approved under this policy.&#10;&#10;MFA adds a check that is separate from knowing the password. It reduces the usefulness of a password learned by another person. It does not make an unexpected sign-in request safe, and staff must still protect passwords and check what they are approving. The relevant control reference is M1032." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-02-23&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-mfa&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;multi-factor-authentication-policy/requirement-and-effective-date&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Multi-Factor Authentication Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/everyday-use/1" label="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/everyday-use/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Multi-Factor Authentication Policy | Multi-Factor Authentication Policy &gt; Everyday use&#10;Approve an MFA request only when you have just started the corresponding sign-in. Check the details shown by the app. If a request appears unexpectedly, do not approve it. Tell Security through a known channel, including when the request appeared and whether you took any action.&#10;&#10;Do not approve a request for someone else, even if they say they are from IT. Do not read an app code to another person. MFA is an individual check and must not become a shared team procedure. Managers must not ask staff to bypass it to meet a deadline." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-02-23&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-mfa&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;multi-factor-authentication-policy/everyday-use&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Multi-Factor Authentication Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/recovery-codes/1" label="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/recovery-codes/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Multi-Factor Authentication Policy | Multi-Factor Authentication Policy &gt; Recovery codes&#10;The authenticator's one-time recovery codes are the fallback when the app cannot be used. Generate them during enrolment and keep them private in a secure place separate from the device used for the app. Each code can be used once. A used code must not be relied upon for a later sign-in.&#10;&#10;Do not send recovery codes by email, put them in the file share or include them in a helpdesk request. The analyst needs to know whether you can use the recovery process, not the code itself. If codes may have been exposed, contact the helpdesk promptly to arrange replacement and tell Security.&#10;&#10;If neither the app nor a recovery code is available, contact the helpdesk for identity verification and recovery assistance. A request for help does not itself authorise removal of MFA. Support must not create an unrecorded exception to complete a call quickly." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-02-23&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-mfa&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;multi-factor-authentication-policy/recovery-codes&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Multi-Factor Authentication Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/exceptions/1" label="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/exceptions/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Multi-Factor Authentication Policy | Multi-Factor Authentication Policy &gt; Exceptions&#10;An exception permits one named account to sign in without MFA. It is allowed only when MFA is technically impossible or an emergency role requires access during an MFA service failure. Convenience, travel and a forgotten phone are not sufficient grounds. First consider whether the normal recovery method solves the problem.&#10;&#10;Thomas Price (thomas.price) must approve every exception. The request must identify the account, explain the technical or emergency need, describe the intended use and propose a review date. Approval is time-limited and must be recorded in the MFA Exception Register before it is applied.&#10;&#10;The register must show the person, username, department, reason, approver, grant date and review date. Security reviews exceptions every quarter and records whether each need still exists. Continued approval requires an explicit decision. An exception must be removed when its justification ends, even if its review date has not arrived." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-02-23&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-mfa&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;multi-factor-authentication-policy/exceptions&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Multi-Factor Authentication Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/ownership-and-checks/1" label="pol-mfa@1.0#multi-factor-authentication-policy/ownership-and-checks/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Multi-Factor Authentication Policy | Multi-Factor Authentication Policy &gt; Ownership and checks&#10;Security owns this policy and the exception review. IT applies approved changes and confirms that the intended account alone is affected. Staff tell the helpdesk about problems early. System owners must not interpret an MFA exception as permission to grant more access; access groups and their approvals remain separate decisions." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-02-23&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-mfa&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;multi-factor-authentication-policy/ownership-and-checks&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Multi-Factor Authentication Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password-2024@2.0#password-policy/purpose-and-application/1" label="pol-password-2024@2.0#password-policy/purpose-and-application/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; Purpose and application&#10;This policy sets the company's password requirements. All staff must follow it when creating or changing passwords for company accounts. Managers must make sure their teams understand the requirements before access is provided. IT Support is responsible for helping people complete password changes and for resolving problems with password entry.&#10;&#10;A password belongs to one person. It must not be shared to cover leave, speed up work or allow a colleague to use a system without their own account. Requests for additional access must follow the access approval process. Permission to do a task does not include permission to use someone else's password." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2024-02-19&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/purpose-and-application&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;superseded&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password-2024@2.0#password-policy/password-composition/1" label="pol-password-2024@2.0#password-policy/password-composition/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; Password composition&#10;Every password must be at least 8 characters long and include an upper case letter, a lower case letter, a digit and a symbol. All four types are required. A password which meets the length rule but omits one of these types is not acceptable.&#10;&#10;Do not choose your name, username, department or an easily guessed word with a single digit added. Avoid dates and information known to colleagues. A password should be difficult for someone else to predict even if they know your job and have seen your public contact details. Choose a value you can remember without displaying it at your desk.&#10;&#10;Do not use the same password for separate systems or for a personal account. If one password becomes known, other accounts must not be exposed by the same value. Where company sign-in is handled by the identity provider, follow that sign-in route rather than creating an unnecessary separate account." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2024-02-19&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/password-composition&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;superseded&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password-2024@2.0#password-policy/scheduled-changes/1" label="pol-password-2024@2.0#password-policy/scheduled-changes/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; Scheduled changes&#10;Passwords must be changed every 90 days. Complete a change when prompted; do not delay it until you next need a system urgently. IT Support can explain the change process if a prompt is unclear. Use the normal sign-in route and do not follow an unexpected email link to make the change.&#10;&#10;The last 5 passwords may not be reused. Select a new password rather than cycling through previous choices. Small predictable variations, such as changing only the final digit, are discouraged. The new value must meet the composition requirements each time.&#10;&#10;Change a password immediately if you believe someone else knows it. A scheduled change date is not a reason to wait. Tell Security about the possible exposure so that it can assess whether further action is required." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2024-02-19&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/scheduled-changes&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;superseded&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password-2024@2.0#password-policy/failed-sign-ins/1" label="pol-password-2024@2.0#password-policy/failed-sign-ins/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; Failed sign-ins&#10;An account is locked after 5 failed sign-in attempts. The lockout lasts 15 minutes. Check the username, keyboard settings and password before trying again after that period. Do not ask someone else to sign in on your behalf while you wait.&#10;&#10;Contact the helpdesk if you cannot complete sign-in or are unsure why it failed. The analyst must verify your identity before carrying out a password reset. Knowing the account name or requesting a reset from a familiar-looking email address is not sufficient by itself." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2024-02-19&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/failed-sign-ins&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;superseded&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password-2024@2.0#password-policy/safe-handling-and-support/1" label="pol-password-2024@2.0#password-policy/safe-handling-and-support/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; Safe handling and support&#10;Do not send a password through email or place it in the file share. Never include it in a helpdesk request. An analyst may ask you to confirm that a change worked, but must not ask you to disclose the new password. Enter passwords privately, including when using a shared machine.&#10;&#10;If a temporary password is issued during a verified reset, use it only to complete the reset and select your own password. Confirm successful access with the helpdesk without repeating the password. IT Support records who requested the reset and the checks performed.&#10;&#10;Managers must allow time for staff to resolve password problems properly. Security deals with suspected exposure and may require further changes. Failure to follow these requirements may lead to access being restricted while the problem is addressed." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2024-02-19&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/safe-handling-and-support&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;superseded&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/purpose/1" label="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/purpose/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; Purpose&#10;A password protects an individual account. It must be difficult to guess and must not be available to another person through sharing or reuse. This policy applies to every company account and to staff in both offices and working remotely. Version 3.0 replaces version 2.0 dated 2024-02-19. Use this version for password creation, changes and helpdesk resets from 2026-06-01.&#10;&#10;Long, unique passwords are the company's standard. Routine calendar changes are no longer required. Change a password when there is reason to suspect exposure, and follow instructions from Security when it directs a change. Do not use a previous policy's routine change timetable for current accounts." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-06-01&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/purpose&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/choosing-a-password/1" label="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/choosing-a-password/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; Choosing a password&#10;Passwords must contain at least 14 characters. Passphrases are encouraged because several unrelated words can be easier to remember than a short complicated string. Choose words that do not form a familiar quotation, a common expression or a description of your own life. A name followed by a date is not a suitable passphrase.&#10;&#10;Do not build passwords from your username, department, the company email domain or information colleagues and customers would know. Adding a punctuation mark to a common password does not make it acceptable. Length matters, but a long predictable phrase can still be guessed.&#10;&#10;Password creation and changes must be checked against the banned-password list. The list includes commonly guessed passwords and passwords known to have been exposed. If a proposed password is rejected, choose a substantially different phrase. Do not keep adding a number until a slight variation is accepted. IT maintains the check with Security's oversight." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-06-01&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/choosing-a-password&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/one-password-for-one-use/1" label="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/one-password-for-one-use/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; One password for one use&#10;Never share a password with a colleague, manager, helpdesk analyst or anyone claiming to provide urgent support. This includes reading it over the phone, sending it in email or allowing another person to watch you type it. An authorised support task does not require the analyst to know your usual password.&#10;&#10;Do not reuse passwords across systems. Do not use a company password for personal services or a personal password for company work. Where a system directs you to the identity provider, use the normal company sign-in process. This is central authentication; it is not an instruction to copy the same password into separate system accounts.&#10;&#10;Do not leave a password on paper beside a laptop or on a shared desk. Do not put it in the file share, an email draft or an ordinary note. If you cannot manage a password safely, contact the helpdesk for guidance before choosing an insecure workaround." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-06-01&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/one-password-for-one-use&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/changes-after-suspected-exposure/1" label="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/changes-after-suspected-exposure/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; Changes after suspected exposure&#10;Tell Security promptly if you think someone else has learned your password, or if you entered it into an unfamiliar prompt. Use a known contact route. Do not continue interacting with the message or page that raised the concern. Security will decide whether further account checks are needed.&#10;&#10;Change the affected password through the normal company route or ask the helpdesk to assist. Choose a new, unrelated password. If the exposed password was used elsewhere, make the other passwords unique as well. A password change alone does not establish that an account is safe; Security may also require sessions to be ended and access to be reviewed." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-06-01&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/changes-after-suspected-exposure&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/helpdesk-resets/1" label="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/helpdesk-resets/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; Helpdesk resets&#10;If you forget your password, contact the helpdesk. The analyst must verify your identity before resetting it, using established staff details and a known contact route. Urgency, seniority and knowledge of a colleague's name do not replace verification. The analyst records the checks completed without recording passwords or recovery codes.&#10;&#10;After verification, the analyst performs the reset and gives instructions for setting a new password privately. Any temporary password is for the reset process only. Select a compliant password and confirm that sign-in works before the request is closed. Do not send the chosen password to the analyst as proof." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-06-01&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/helpdesk-resets&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/responsibilities/1" label="pol-password@3.0#password-policy/responsibilities/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Policy | Password Policy &gt; Responsibilities&#10;Staff follow these rules and report possible exposure. IT Support applies the verification procedure consistently and records the outcome of resets. Security reviews the banned-password requirements and handles suspected account misuse. System owners must raise any system limitation with Security rather than silently accepting weaker passwords. MFA remains a separate requirement under the Multi-Factor Authentication Policy." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-06-01&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-password&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-policy/responsibilities&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: &quot;pol-password-2024&quot;, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/scope/1" label="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/scope/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Remote Access and VPN Policy | Remote Access and VPN Policy &gt; Scope&#10;This policy covers access to company systems from outside the UK office and the US office. Remote access must use the approved sign-in route and a company laptop. Working from home does not change the permissions a person has, the sensitivity of information or the requirement to protect it from other people nearby.&#10;&#10;Staff may sign in to email and the identity provider from home on a company laptop. Access to other internal systems from outside the offices must go through the VPN gateway. Use a company laptop for those connections. Permission to connect does not confer permission to administer the VPN gateway or use systems outside your approved access groups." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-04-20&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-remote-access&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Infrastructure&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;remote-access-and-vpn-policy/scope&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Remote Access and VPN Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/starting-a-remote-session/1" label="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/starting-a-remote-session/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Remote Access and VPN Policy | Remote Access and VPN Policy &gt; Starting a remote session&#10;Use your own company account and the company laptop issued to you. Start the VPN connection through the normal company setup, then complete sign-in and the required MFA check. If the authenticator app cannot be used, follow the recovery-code procedure in the Multi-Factor Authentication Policy.&#10;&#10;Check that the connection is established before opening another internal system. If it cannot be established, contact the helpdesk. Do not move company information to a personal device or ask a colleague to send restricted material through another route to get around the connection problem.&#10;&#10;All Staff membership allows ordinary use of the VPN gateway. Administrative access to the VPN gateway is restricted to IT Admins and is subject to system owner approval. Leah Gibbons (leah.gibbons) owns the VPN gateway. Questions about its configuration must go through IT Infrastructure rather than being resolved by changing settings without approval." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-04-20&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-remote-access&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Infrastructure&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;remote-access-and-vpn-policy/starting-a-remote-session&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Remote Access and VPN Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/addresses-and-attribution/1" label="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/addresses-and-attribution/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Remote Access and VPN Policy | Remote Access and VPN Policy &gt; Addresses and attribution&#10;The VPN gateway assigns addresses from a small shared pool in turn. Several people can use the same address during a week. An address identifies a session in context; it is not a permanent label for a person or laptop.&#10;&#10;When investigating remote access, record the session time in UTC, the account and the device as well as the address shown in the connection records. Compare the relevant time periods before connecting activity to a person. Two accounts using an address at different times are not evidence that the people shared credentials or a device.&#10;&#10;Support staff must explain this limitation when passing information to Security. Do not identify someone solely from an address copied from a separate day's record. If the session cannot be matched confidently, say that attribution is unresolved and request the missing information." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-04-20&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-remote-access&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Infrastructure&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;remote-access-and-vpn-policy/addresses-and-attribution&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Remote Access and VPN Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/working-abroad/1" label="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/working-abroad/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Remote Access and VPN Policy | Remote Access and VPN Policy &gt; Working abroad&#10;File a travel notice with Security before travelling abroad for work. Include the destination country, travel dates, purpose, company laptop name and the manager's approval. Security acknowledges the notice so that expected overseas sign-ins can be checked against it.&#10;&#10;Tell Security if the country, dates or laptop changes. A notice for one trip does not cover later journeys. A confirmed trip can explain a new country in a sign-in record, but it does not excuse unexpected devices or activity outside the travel period. Security may contact the traveller through a known route to clarify those details.&#10;&#10;Use the same VPN requirements abroad as at home. Take reasonable care when working where others can see the screen. Do not leave the company laptop unattended in a public place. Report loss to the helpdesk the same day and give the last known location and time." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-04-20&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-remote-access&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Infrastructure&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;remote-access-and-vpn-policy/working-abroad&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Remote Access and VPN Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/personal-phones/1" label="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/personal-phones/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Remote Access and VPN Policy | Remote Access and VPN Policy &gt; Personal phones&#10;Personal phones may be used to read email only. They are not company devices and are not registered. This permission does not authorise access to the intranet, file share or any other internal system from a personal phone. Do not copy attachments into personal services or use a personal phone as a substitute for a company laptop.&#10;&#10;Protect email on the phone from casual access by others. Report suspected exposure through the normal support or Security contact route. When checking email activity, an unregistered personal phone may be consistent with this limited permission; it must not be treated as a registered company laptop." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-04-20&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-remote-access&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Infrastructure&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;remote-access-and-vpn-policy/personal-phones&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Remote Access and VPN Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/ending-work-and-support/1" label="pol-remote-access@1.4#remote-access-and-vpn-policy/ending-work-and-support/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Remote Access and VPN Policy | Remote Access and VPN Policy &gt; Ending work and support&#10;End remote sessions when work is finished. Keep the laptop secure between sessions and follow IT instructions for maintenance. Report connection problems to the helpdesk with the time in UTC and the laptop name. Never include passwords or recovery codes. IT Infrastructure owns the connection process, and Security handles questions about possible account misuse." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-04-20&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;pol-remote-access&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Infrastructure&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;remote-access-and-vpn-policy/ending-work-and-support&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Remote Access and VPN Policy&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="rec-approved-travel@1.0#travel-notice-maya-patel-germany/travel-details/1" label="rec-approved-travel@1.0#travel-notice-maya-patel-germany/travel-details/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Travel Notice: Maya Patel, Germany | Travel Notice: Maya Patel, Germany &gt; Travel details&#10;Traveller: Maya Patel (maya.patel), Sales, UK office.&#10;&#10;Destination: Germany.&#10;&#10;Travel dates: 2026-10-02 to 2026-10-04, inclusive.&#10;&#10;Purpose: customer meetings. Maya will take her assigned company laptop, LT-MPATEL, for meeting preparation, email and access to company work while away. The notice covers this journey only. A change in country, dates or laptop must be sent to Security before the changed arrangements are used." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-09-25&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;Sales&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;rec-approved-travel&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;travel-notice-maya-patel-germany/travel-details&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Travel Notice: Maya Patel, Germany&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="rec-approved-travel@1.0#travel-notice-maya-patel-germany/approval-and-acknowledgement/1" label="rec-approved-travel@1.0#travel-notice-maya-patel-germany/approval-and-acknowledgement/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Travel Notice: Maya Patel, Germany | Travel Notice: Maya Patel, Germany &gt; Approval and acknowledgement&#10;Callum Reeves (callum.reeves) approved the trip on 2026-09-25. The business purpose and travel dates were confirmed with the traveller. No additional system permissions were requested as part of this notice.&#10;&#10;Thomas Price (thomas.price) acknowledged the travel notice for Security on 2026-09-25. His note: sign-ins from Germany on 2026-10-02 to 2026-10-04 are expected for Maya Patel. Use LT-MPATEL as the expected company laptop when checking activity during this period." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-09-25&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;Sales&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;rec-approved-travel&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;travel-notice-maya-patel-germany/approval-and-acknowledgement&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Travel Notice: Maya Patel, Germany&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="rec-approved-travel@1.0#travel-notice-maya-patel-germany/instructions-to-the-traveller/1" label="rec-approved-travel@1.0#travel-notice-maya-patel-germany/instructions-to-the-traveller/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Travel Notice: Maya Patel, Germany | Travel Notice: Maya Patel, Germany &gt; Instructions to the traveller&#10;Use the normal company sign-in process and MFA. Reach internal systems through the VPN gateway as required by the Remote Access and VPN Policy. Keep the laptop under your control and report any loss to the helpdesk the same day.&#10;&#10;Security may contact Maya through an established contact route if a sign-in needs clarification. This notice supplies travel context; it does not establish that every action during the trip was performed by Maya. Activity outside the stated dates or from a different device needs its own checks. All recorded contact or sign-in times associated with this notice are to be expressed in UTC." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-09-25&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;Sales&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;rec-approved-travel&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;travel-notice-maya-patel-germany/instructions-to-the-traveller&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Travel Notice: Maya Patel, Germany&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-device-onboarding@2.0#device-onboarding-and-replacement/replacement-steps/1" label="run-device-onboarding@2.0#device-onboarding-and-replacement/replacement-steps/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Device Onboarding and Replacement | Device Onboarding and Replacement &gt; Replacement steps&#10;5. **Record the reason and both names.** Describe the fault in ordinary words. The handover record must include the recipient, original laptop name, replacement laptop name, exact handover time in UTC and analyst. James Carter (james.carter), for example, uses LT-JCARTER as the original name and LT-JCARTER-R2 as the replacement name. Do not record only the new name; the relationship between the two matters when later activity is checked.&#10;&#10;6. **Take the original laptop back into IT custody.** The old laptop goes back to IT for repair. Record its condition and physical return. It is no longer the employee's working laptop after the replacement handover. If the original cannot be returned at the appointment, record the reason and arrange collection rather than marking it received.&#10;&#10;7. **Complete the handover together.** The recipient confirms receipt of the replacement and the return of the original. The analyst confirms the names, time and condition. Both sign the handover record. Check that the person can use the replacement for normal work before closing the support request. Explain where to report any remaining problem." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-26&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-device-onboarding&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;device-onboarding-and-replacement/replacement-steps&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Device Onboarding and Replacement&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="rec-helpdesk-reset@1.0#helpdesk-ticket-sd-48213-password-reset/ticket-details/1" label="rec-helpdesk-reset@1.0#helpdesk-ticket-sd-48213-password-reset/ticket-details/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Helpdesk Ticket SD-48213: Password Reset | Helpdesk Ticket SD-48213: Password Reset &gt; Ticket details&#10;Ticket: SD-48213. Date: 2026-10-05. Category: password reset. Status: closed.&#10;&#10;Caller: Oliver Hayes (oliver.hayes), Sales, UK office. Analyst: Joseph Burton (joseph.burton), IT Support. Device: LT-OHAYES. Working location: home.&#10;&#10;Oliver said he had changed his password on the evening of 2026-10-04 and could not remember the new value. He continued trying to sign in while contacting the helpdesk. The request was handled as a forgotten-password problem." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-10-05&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;rec-helpdesk-reset&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;helpdesk-ticket-sd-48213-password-reset/ticket-details&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Helpdesk Ticket SD-48213: Password Reset&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="rec-helpdesk-reset@1.0#helpdesk-ticket-sd-48213-password-reset/timeline/1" label="rec-helpdesk-reset@1.0#helpdesk-ticket-sd-48213-password-reset/timeline/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Helpdesk Ticket SD-48213: Password Reset | Helpdesk Ticket SD-48213: Password Reset &gt; Timeline&#10;All times below are UTC on 2026-10-05.&#10;&#10;| Time | Ticket entry |&#10;|---|---|&#10;| 06:00 to 06:09 UTC | Twenty failed attempts, one every thirty seconds. The attempt sequence began at 06:00:00 UTC and ended at 06:09:30 UTC, within the recorded minute window. |&#10;| 06:05 UTC | Oliver phoned the helpdesk while still trying to sign in. He described the previous evening's password change and identified LT-OHAYES as the laptop in use. |&#10;| 06:07 UTC | Joseph began identity verification through the established staff contact details. Oliver was asked to stop guessing and follow the analyst's instructions. |&#10;| About 06:09 UTC | Identity verification completed. Joseph reset the password after the final failed attempt and guided Oliver through setting a new password privately. |&#10;| 06:10 UTC | Oliver signed in successfully with the new password and confirmed that he could access email. |&#10;| 06:13 UTC | Joseph confirmed the outcome with Oliver and closed SD-48213. |" />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-10-05&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;rec-helpdesk-reset&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;helpdesk-ticket-sd-48213-password-reset/timeline&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Helpdesk Ticket SD-48213: Password Reset&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="rec-helpdesk-reset@1.0#helpdesk-ticket-sd-48213-password-reset/resolution-and-closure/1" label="rec-helpdesk-reset@1.0#helpdesk-ticket-sd-48213-password-reset/resolution-and-closure/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Helpdesk Ticket SD-48213: Password Reset | Helpdesk Ticket SD-48213: Password Reset &gt; Resolution and closure&#10;Identity verification was completed before the reset. No password or recovery code was written into the ticket. The successful sign-in followed the helpdesk reset and was confirmed by the caller during the same call.&#10;&#10;Joseph reminded Oliver to choose a unique passphrase of at least 14 characters and to contact the helpdesk when a password cannot be remembered. No device replacement was needed. Closed by Joseph Burton after Oliver confirmed normal access on LT-OHAYES." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-10-05&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;rec-helpdesk-reset&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;helpdesk-ticket-sd-48213-password-reset/resolution-and-closure&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Helpdesk Ticket SD-48213: Password Reset&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="rec-laptop-handover@1.0#laptop-handover-record-james-carter/recipient-and-reason/1" label="rec-laptop-handover@1.0#laptop-handover-record-james-carter/recipient-and-reason/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Laptop Handover Record: James Carter | Laptop Handover Record: James Carter &gt; Recipient and reason&#10;Recipient: James Carter (james.carter), Sales, UK office.&#10;&#10;Issuing analyst: Ruby Stevens (ruby.stevens), IT Support.&#10;&#10;James reported a broken screen hinge on LT-JCARTER. IT Support arranged a replacement so he could continue normal work while the original laptop was assessed for repair. This record concerns the device assignment and physical handover." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-10-04&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;rec-laptop-handover&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;laptop-handover-record-james-carter/recipient-and-reason&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Laptop Handover Record: James Carter&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="rec-laptop-handover@1.0#laptop-handover-record-james-carter/handover-details/1" label="rec-laptop-handover@1.0#laptop-handover-record-james-carter/handover-details/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Laptop Handover Record: James Carter | Laptop Handover Record: James Carter &gt; Handover details&#10;| Item | Recorded value |&#10;|---|---|&#10;| Date | 2026-10-04 |&#10;| Handover time | 12:00 UTC |&#10;| Original laptop | LT-JCARTER |&#10;| Replacement laptop | LT-JCARTER-R2 |&#10;| Original condition | Screen hinge broken |&#10;| Original destination | Returned to IT for repair |&#10;| Working laptop from handover | LT-JCARTER-R2 |&#10;&#10;Ruby handed LT-JCARTER-R2 to James at 12:00 UTC and received LT-JCARTER into IT custody at the same handover. James began using LT-JCARTER-R2 from that point. The original is not his working laptop while it is with IT for repair." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-10-04&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;rec-laptop-handover&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;laptop-handover-record-james-carter/handover-details&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Laptop Handover Record: James Carter&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="rec-laptop-handover@1.0#laptop-handover-record-james-carter/checks-and-acknowledgement/1" label="rec-laptop-handover@1.0#laptop-handover-record-james-carter/checks-and-acknowledgement/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Laptop Handover Record: James Carter | Laptop Handover Record: James Carter &gt; Checks and acknowledgement&#10;James checked the replacement's screen and keyboard and completed his normal sign-in privately. Ruby confirmed the device name and that ordinary work access was available. This replacement does not change James's account or approve any additional access.&#10;&#10;For any later device enquiry, compare the activity time with this handover. The replacement name is expected for James from 12:00 UTC on 2026-10-04. Any future return to a repaired laptop will require a separate assignment record." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-10-04&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;rec-laptop-handover&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;laptop-handover-record-james-carter/checks-and-acknowledgement&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Laptop Handover Record: James Carter&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="rec-laptop-handover@1.0#laptop-handover-record-james-carter/signatures/1" label="rec-laptop-handover@1.0#laptop-handover-record-james-carter/signatures/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Laptop Handover Record: James Carter | Laptop Handover Record: James Carter &gt; Signatures&#10;Recipient signature: James Carter. Signed 2026-10-04 at 12:00 UTC. Replacement received and original returned.&#10;&#10;IT Support signature: Ruby Stevens. Signed 2026-10-04 at 12:00 UTC. Replacement issued and original accepted for repair." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-10-04&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;rec-laptop-handover&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;laptop-handover-record-james-carter/signatures&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Laptop Handover Record: James Carter&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="reg-mfa-exceptions@1.6#mfa-exception-register/active-exceptions/1" label="reg-mfa-exceptions@1.6#mfa-exception-register/active-exceptions/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="MFA Exception Register | MFA Exception Register &gt; Active exceptions&#10;Register custodian: Isabel Knight (isabel.knight), Security. Approving authority: Thomas Price (thomas.price). The table lists the complete set of active exceptions following the quarterly review on 2026-09-30. There are two active entries. Each permits the named account to sign in without MFA for the approved reason; neither entry grants additional system access.&#10;&#10;| Person | Username | Department | Reason | Approver | Date granted | Review date |&#10;|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|&#10;| Daniel Hughes (daniel.hughes) | daniel.hughes | Engineering | Works on a lab workstation where the authenticator app cannot be installed | Thomas Price | 2026-04-14 | 2026-12-31 |&#10;| Amelia Ward (amelia.ward) | amelia.ward | IT | Holds the emergency administrator role for the identity provider, so she can recover access if the MFA service fails | Thomas Price | 2026-03-02 | 2026-12-31 |" />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-09-30&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;reg-mfa-exceptions&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;register&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;mfa-exception-register/active-exceptions&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;MFA Exception Register&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="reg-mfa-exceptions@1.6#mfa-exception-register/quarterly-review-note/1" label="reg-mfa-exceptions@1.6#mfa-exception-register/quarterly-review-note/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="MFA Exception Register | MFA Exception Register &gt; Quarterly review note&#10;The last quarterly review was completed on 2026-09-30. Thomas Price confirmed that both recorded needs still apply. The lab restriction remains unresolved, and the emergency administrator role still requires a route for recovering access during an MFA service failure. Both approvals stand until the end of 2026 and must be reconsidered by 2026-12-31.&#10;&#10;Security checks whether each reason remains necessary, whether a workable MFA method is available and whether the named person still holds the relevant duties. A previous approval is not an automatic extension. A change in need must be reported immediately rather than held until the next scheduled review." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-09-30&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;reg-mfa-exceptions&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;register&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;mfa-exception-register/quarterly-review-note&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;MFA Exception Register&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="reg-mfa-exceptions@1.6#mfa-exception-register/use-of-the-register/1" label="reg-mfa-exceptions@1.6#mfa-exception-register/use-of-the-register/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="MFA Exception Register | MFA Exception Register &gt; Use of the register&#10;When checking a sign-in, match the username and date to the entry. Do not assume that everyone in the same department has the same exception. Record whether MFA was completed separately from whether an exception existed. The register establishes approval context; it does not establish who performed a particular sign-in.&#10;&#10;Any amendment requires Thomas Price's approval and a recorded review date. IT confirms implementation to Security. Routine recovery problems should first follow the authenticator recovery-code and helpdesk process." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-09-30&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;reg-mfa-exceptions&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;register&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;mfa-exception-register/use-of-the-register&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;MFA Exception Register&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="reg-shared-devices@1.3#shared-device-register/use-and-responsibility/1" label="reg-shared-devices@1.3#shared-device-register/use-and-responsibility/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Shared Device Register | Shared Device Register &gt; Use and responsibility&#10;The meeting-room PC is available to its listed users for meeting preparation and presentation work. Leave the desk clear and end the account session after use. Do not assume the next person is allowed to view the information left on screen. Report equipment faults to the helpdesk using the device name.&#10;&#10;The warehouse terminal supports work in the warehouse area. The same individual-account rule applies even when users change over quickly. A person's presence in this table does not grant access to another person's work or increase their system permissions. Access continues to depend on their own approved groups." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-06-15&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;reg-shared-devices&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;register&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;shared-device-register/use-and-responsibility&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Shared Device Register&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="reg-shared-devices@1.3#shared-device-register/review-arrangements/1" label="reg-shared-devices@1.3#shared-device-register/review-arrangements/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Shared Device Register | Shared Device Register &gt; Review arrangements&#10;IT Support reviews this register twice a year. The next routine review is due in December 2026. Confirm the physical location, the people who still need the machine and any change in how it is used. Location changes and requests to add or remove users should be sent to IT Support when they arise.&#10;&#10;For a sign-in enquiry, check the actual session time and account alongside this register. The user list describes expected use; it is not a record of who used the device at a particular moment. Ask the relevant team to clarify a session when that detail is needed." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-06-15&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;reg-shared-devices&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;register&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;shared-device-register/review-arrangements&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Shared Device Register&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-compromised-account-response@1.3#compromised-account-response/purpose-and-responsibility/1" label="run-compromised-account-response@1.3#compromised-account-response/purpose-and-responsibility/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Compromised Account Response | Compromised Account Response &gt; Purpose and responsibility&#10;Use this procedure when an account is believed to be in the wrong hands. It applies to ordinary staff accounts and privileged accounts. Security coordinates the work. IT applies the account changes, and affected system owners help assess what the account could reach. Reference T1078 where an investigation needs a classification; T1078.002 may apply to domain account use when the evidence supports it.&#10;&#10;Open a working record with an SD- reference. Record the account, the reason for concern, the assigned analyst and action times in UTC. Distinguish what has been observed from what is still being checked. Do not wait for every detail before taking necessary containment action." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-07-14&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-compromised-account-response&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;compromised-account-response/purpose-and-responsibility&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [&quot;T1078&quot;, &quot;T1078.002&quot;], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Compromised Account Response&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-compromised-account-response@1.3#compromised-account-response/response-steps/1" label="run-compromised-account-response@1.3#compromised-account-response/response-steps/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Compromised Account Response | Compromised Account Response &gt; Response steps&#10;1. **Disable sign-in.** Ask an authorised IT Admins member to disable sign-in for the account. Record the person making the change and the completion time. Confirm that the action applied to the intended account. Tell the account holder through a known contact route that access is being handled by Security. Avoid relying on the account under investigation to establish who is responding.&#10;&#10;2. **Revoke sessions.** End active sessions and check the outcome. Disabling a new sign-in does not by itself demonstrate that an existing session has ended. Record any session that cannot be revoked and contact the affected system owner for further action. Make the remaining exposure clear to the security lead.&#10;&#10;3. **Reset the password.** Arrange a password reset using the helpdesk's identity verification process. The new password must follow the current Password Policy. Do not send it through the account being investigated or include it in the working record. Completing a reset does not authorise re-enabling sign-in. Keep that decision separate until the investigation and recovery checks are complete.&#10;&#10;4. **Review sign-ins, devices and addresses.** Set a time window and examine recent activity, including successes and failures. Compare device names with the person's issued company laptop and any handover record. Check approved travel before treating a country change as unexplained. Record addresses in the investigation evidence where available, together with their session times. The VPN gateway reuses a small address pool, so an address without its time is insufficient attribution." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-07-14&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-compromised-account-response&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;compromised-account-response/response-steps&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Compromised Account Response&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-new-country-sign-in@1.0#new-country-sign-in-triage/recording-an-expected-journey/1" label="run-new-country-sign-in@1.0#new-country-sign-in-triage/recording-an-expected-journey/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="New-Country Sign-In Triage | New-Country Sign-In Triage &gt; Recording an expected journey&#10;A conclusion of expected travel applies to the assessed activity. It must not suppress later checks for different dates, devices or countries. If the traveller extends or changes the trip, ask them to update Security through the normal travel notice process. Security records the outcome in plain language and closes the enquiry only when the relevant checks are complete." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-08-03&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-new-country-sign-in&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;new-country-sign-in-triage/recording-an-expected-journey&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;New-Country Sign-In Triage&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-compromised-account-response@1.3#compromised-account-response/response-steps/2" label="run-compromised-account-response@1.3#compromised-account-response/response-steps/2">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Compromised Account Response | Compromised Account Response &gt; Response steps&#10;5. **List groups and reachable systems.** Establish membership during the period under review. All Staff reaches email, intranet and file share. IT Admins permits administration of the identity provider, VPN gateway and domain controller. Finance Approvers reaches the payments system and ERP. HR Data Owners reaches the HR system. Ask the owners of reachable systems to check relevant activity. Do not assume a person's department tells you which privileges they hold.&#10;&#10;6. **Check related accounts.** Identify other accounts that used the same device or address in the same period. For shared machines, consult the Shared Device Register and confirm whether its entries are still accurate. For the VPN gateway, compare session allocation times before drawing a connection. Investigate a related account on its own evidence. Shared equipment or an address match is a reason to check, not proof that another account was misused.&#10;&#10;7. **Review any MFA exception.** Check the MFA Exception Register for the account, approval, reason and review date. Establish whether sign-in occurred under that exception or with completed MFA. Ask whether the approved need still applies. Escalate any proposed exception change to Thomas Price (thomas.price). Do not leave an exception in place simply because it predates the concern.&#10;&#10;8. **Prepare restoration and obtain sign-off.** Confirm that the account holder's identity has been verified, the password has changed, sessions have been revoked and the device is suitable for use. Confirm group memberships and the required MFA setup or approved exception. Summarise outstanding uncertainty for Thomas Price. Restore access only after his explicit sign-off, then record who restored it and when." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-07-14&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-compromised-account-response&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;compromised-account-response/response-steps&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Compromised Account Response&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-compromised-account-response@1.3#compromised-account-response/follow-through/1" label="run-compromised-account-response@1.3#compromised-account-response/follow-through/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Compromised Account Response | Compromised Account Response &gt; Follow-through&#10;After restoration, ask the person to complete a normal sign-in and confirm that the required access works. Check for unexpected activity during the agreed observation period. Keep staff instructions practical and avoid sharing speculative conclusions. Use the Incident Communication Template when a wider message is needed. Record open tasks with an owner and a due date so that restored access does not cause unfinished checks to be overlooked." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-07-14&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-compromised-account-response&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;compromised-account-response/follow-through&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Compromised Account Response&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-device-onboarding@2.0#device-onboarding-and-replacement/purpose-and-preparation/1" label="run-device-onboarding@2.0#device-onboarding-and-replacement/purpose-and-preparation/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Device Onboarding and Replacement | Device Onboarding and Replacement &gt; Purpose and preparation&#10;Use this procedure when issuing a company laptop to a starter or replacing a damaged laptop. IT Support owns the handover. The analyst must know who will receive the laptop, their department and office, and whether this is a first issue or a replacement. Do not give out a laptop against an unverified request.&#10;&#10;Check the person's account arrangements before the appointment. The laptop handover does not approve additional access. All Staff is the baseline, and privileged membership needs system owner approval under the Access Control Policy. The access check is consistent with M1018: confirm the permissions needed for the role rather than copying a colleague's membership." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-26&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-device-onboarding&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;device-onboarding-and-replacement/purpose-and-preparation&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Device Onboarding and Replacement&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-device-onboarding@2.0#device-onboarding-and-replacement/issue-steps/1" label="run-device-onboarding@2.0#device-onboarding-and-replacement/issue-steps/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Device Onboarding and Replacement | Device Onboarding and Replacement &gt; Issue steps&#10;1. **Identify the person and prepare the laptop.** Confirm the recipient against the starter or replacement request. Check that the laptop is ready for company use and that its physical condition is suitable. Resolve visible damage before handover. Do not ask the person to use an unprepared personal device while the company laptop is being arranged.&#10;&#10;2. **Apply the naming rule.** A laptop name starts with LT-, followed by the person's first initial and surname in capitals. For example, Maya Patel (maya.patel) has LT-MPATEL. Use the spelling in the staff details and check the name before completing the device record. A replacement adds -R2 to the original laptop name. Do not invent another naming pattern to complete a request more quickly.&#10;&#10;3. **Record the assignment.** Record the person, username, department, office, laptop name, issue date, time in UTC and the analyst. Explain that the laptop is assigned to one person. It must not be passed to a colleague without IT Support arranging the change. Confirm that the device record and the physical laptop refer to the same name.&#10;&#10;4. **Support first sign-in.** Ask the person to sign in through the identity provider and complete the password and MFA steps privately. Verify that ordinary email, intranet and file share access works. Where approved privileged membership is required, check only the access that has been authorised. The analyst must not request the person's chosen password or recovery codes." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-26&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-device-onboarding&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;device-onboarding-and-replacement/issue-steps&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Device Onboarding and Replacement&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-device-onboarding@2.0#device-onboarding-and-replacement/shared-machines-and-completion/1" label="run-device-onboarding@2.0#device-onboarding-and-replacement/shared-machines-and-completion/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Device Onboarding and Replacement | Device Onboarding and Replacement &gt; Shared machines and completion&#10;MR-PC-01 and WH-TERM-01 are shared machines. Their locations and authorised users belong in the Shared Device Register. Do not treat either machine as a person's individually issued laptop. Report changes to their location or users to IT Support for review.&#10;&#10;Before closure, confirm that the assignment, handover and any repair action agree. Device history must make clear which laptop a person was using at a particular time. A different name after a documented replacement is expected and should be explainable from the handover record." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-05-26&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-device-onboarding&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;IT Support&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;device-onboarding-and-replacement/shared-machines-and-completion&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Device Onboarding and Replacement&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-new-country-sign-in@1.0#new-country-sign-in-triage/purpose/1" label="run-new-country-sign-in@1.0#new-country-sign-in-triage/purpose/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="New-Country Sign-In Triage | New-Country Sign-In Triage &gt; Purpose&#10;Use this procedure for a successful sign-in from a country the account has not used before. A new country is a reason to establish context. It does not by itself show that the account has been misused. Security owns the decision and may ask IT Support to confirm device details or help contact the person.&#10;&#10;Record the account, country, successful sign-in time in UTC and device name. Note whether the country comes from a sign-in record or another source. Do not describe the country estimate as proof of a person's physical location. T1078 may be relevant if subsequent checks support possible use of valid credentials by someone else." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-08-03&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-new-country-sign-in&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;new-country-sign-in-triage/purpose&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [&quot;T1078&quot;], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;New-Country Sign-In Triage&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-new-country-sign-in@1.0#new-country-sign-in-triage/triage-steps/1" label="run-new-country-sign-in@1.0#new-country-sign-in-triage/triage-steps/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="New-Country Sign-In Triage | New-Country Sign-In Triage &gt; Triage steps&#10;1. **Check travel notices.** Look for an approved notice covering the person, country and dates. Read the acknowledgement as well as the request. Confirm that the period includes the sign-in time. A notice for a neighbouring country or an earlier trip is not a match. If a notice is missing or incomplete, record the gap and continue the checks rather than assuming travel was approved.&#10;&#10;2. **Check the company laptop.** Compare the sign-in device with the person's own issued laptop. Consult the current device record and any replacement handover. A replacement may legitimately have a different name from the original laptop. Confirm the effective handover time before deciding that an unfamiliar name is unexplained. A shared machine is not the person's own laptop. A personal phone is not registered company equipment, although reading email on it is permitted.&#10;&#10;3. **Contact the person through a known channel.** Use contact details already established by the company. Do not rely on a reply from the session being checked or on new contact details offered during the enquiry. Ask whether they are in the country, whether they made the sign-in at the recorded time and which device they used. Never ask for their password, authenticator code or recovery code. Record the response and the time of contact in UTC.&#10;&#10;4. **Compare all the evidence.** Check that the notice, device and person's confirmation agree. An approved journey explains the country only within its dates. It does not explain a device the traveller denies using. Also consider whether the sign-in route matches the Remote Access and VPN Policy. For VPN activity, match the session time because the gateway assigns addresses from a shared pool. Do not attach a different person's session to the traveller solely because an address matches." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-08-03&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-new-country-sign-in&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;new-country-sign-in-triage/triage-steps&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;New-Country Sign-In Triage&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-new-country-sign-in@1.0#new-country-sign-in-triage/triage-steps/2" label="run-new-country-sign-in@1.0#new-country-sign-in-triage/triage-steps/2">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="New-Country Sign-In Triage | New-Country Sign-In Triage &gt; Triage steps&#10;5. **Decide and record the outcome.** Treat the sign-in as expected travel when the checks support that conclusion. State which notice, device evidence and contact confirmation support it. If travel is not confirmed, or the person denies the activity, treat the account as possibly misused and hand it over to the Compromised Account Response procedure. An unanswered call is not confirmation of travel. Escalate promptly rather than leaving the case indefinitely waiting for a reply.&#10;&#10;6. **Complete the handover where required.** Provide the account, time, country, device, travel check and contact attempts. Identify which facts are confirmed and which remain uncertain. Include any containment already applied so that the next analyst knows the current access state. The receiving analyst should acknowledge responsibility for further action." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-08-03&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-new-country-sign-in&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;new-country-sign-in-triage/triage-steps&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;New-Country Sign-In Triage&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-password-spray-response@1.1#password-spray-response/when-to-use-this-procedure/1" label="run-password-spray-response@1.1#password-spray-response/when-to-use-this-procedure/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Spray Response | Password Spray Response &gt; When to use this procedure&#10;Use this procedure when one source appears to be trying one or a few common passwords against many accounts. Repeated failures against one account are not enough to establish this pattern. Security leads the assessment, with IT helping to apply access changes and obtain the relevant sign-in records.&#10;&#10;Record observations and action times in UTC. Separate confirmed facts from assumptions. A source address can be shared, particularly through the VPN gateway, so do not assume that it identifies one employee. Keep the scope focused on the observed activity and expand it when evidence supports doing so." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-07-14&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-password-spray-response&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-spray-response/when-to-use-this-procedure&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Spray Response&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-password-spray-response@1.1#password-spray-response/response-steps/1" label="run-password-spray-response@1.1#password-spray-response/response-steps/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Spray Response | Password Spray Response &gt; Response steps&#10;1. **Confirm the pattern across accounts.** Compare attempts from the source over a defined time window. Count distinct accounts, note the timing and compare the failure reasons. Establish whether the behaviour is consistent with one or a few common passwords being tried broadly. Do not collect or reproduce attempted password values. If the records do not establish the pattern, record the uncertainty and investigate other explanations, including an ordinary password problem.&#10;&#10;2. **Block the source.** Ask the responsible administrator to block the observed source at the relevant access boundary. Record who applied the block, when it took effect and how it was checked. Consider whether it is a shared company connection so that the impact on legitimate staff is understood. If a precise block cannot be applied, escalate immediately to Thomas Price (thomas.price) for a containment decision. Do not treat a requested block as completed until it has been verified.&#10;&#10;3. **Check for successful attempts.** Search the same source and time window for successes, not just failures. Include activity immediately before and after the visible failure pattern where useful. List each account with a successful result and the corresponding time and device. A successful sign-in is a priority for investigation even if the account had many earlier failures. A source block does not end a session already created.&#10;&#10;4. **Check MFA for each success.** Establish whether the sign-in included completed MFA. Check the MFA Exception Register for the named account and the approval period. Distinguish a completed challenge from a prompt merely being issued. If the account had an exception, identify its scope and justification. Neither an exception nor a completed challenge proves that the person using the account was authorised." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-07-14&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-password-spray-response&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-spray-response/response-steps&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Spray Response&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-password-spray-response@1.1#password-spray-response/response-steps/2" label="run-password-spray-response@1.1#password-spray-response/response-steps/2">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Spray Response | Password Spray Response &gt; Response steps&#10;5. **Reset passwords and revoke sessions.** For accounts with a success in the suspected pattern, arrange password resets and revoke active sessions. Use the verified helpdesk process when contacting staff. Record the completion of both actions separately. Do not ask a person to supply their existing password, app code or recovery code. Accounts without a success still need assessment; decide any additional action from the evidence rather than assuming all have been used.&#10;&#10;6. **Check reachable systems.** List each affected account's actual group memberships. All Staff reaches email, intranet and file share. IT Admins provides administration of the identity provider, VPN gateway and domain controller. Finance Approvers reaches the payments system and ERP. HR Data Owners reaches the HR system. Use the membership at the relevant time where available and identify gaps in that evidence.&#10;&#10;7. **Escalate account misuse.** Hand any account with a suspected unauthorised success to the Compromised Account Response procedure. Pass the source, time window, accounts, devices, MFA findings, group memberships and completed containment actions. Identify the person taking responsibility for the next steps. Do not close the assessment simply because the source has stopped trying." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-07-14&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-password-spray-response&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-spray-response/response-steps&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Spray Response&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
      <node id="run-password-spray-response@1.1#password-spray-response/completion-checks/1" label="run-password-spray-response@1.1#password-spray-response/completion-checks/1">
        <viz:color r="127" g="127" b="127" />
        <attvalues>
          <attvalue for="5" value="chunk" />
          <attvalue for="15" value="Password Spray Response | Password Spray Response &gt; Completion checks&#10;Security confirms that the block outcome, success search and account handovers are documented. If no success was found, state the records and time window examined. Describe any missing evidence plainly. Thomas Price decides whether further investigation or staff communication is needed. Use the Incident Communication Template if staff action is required." />
          <attvalue for="16" value="{&quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-07-14&quot;, &quot;department&quot;: &quot;IT&quot;, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;run-password-spray-response&quot;, &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;Security&quot;, &quot;section_path&quot;: &quot;password-spray-response/completion-checks&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;current&quot;, &quot;supersedes&quot;: null, &quot;technique_ids&quot;: [], &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Password Spray Response&quot;}" />
        </attvalues>
      </node>
    </nodes>
